Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  familiar slogans from the protests of  2018 and 2019  once again echo across sudan in the  capital cartoon hundreds took to the  streets to express their anger at what  they say is an attempt by the military  to disrupt the country's transition to  democracy  we were expecting a speedy transition to  civilian rule we don't actually feel  there is a transition to anything here  yet today there is no freedom peace or  justice in sudan  between sudan's military and civilian  politicians reached a low point this  week  after the recent attempted coup  when it took power two years ago the  transitional government promised to fix  decades of mismanagement internal  conflict and international sanctions  under the former president umar  al-bashir but it's yet to demonstrate to  the people of sudan that it can undo  damage done by bashir's regime  kick-start the country's struggling  economy and move the country towards  genuine democratic governance  what we need is to revamp and strengthen  government institutions especially our  weak institutions of justice only then  would we stop worrying about who rules  sudan  against all odds and most predictions  sudan's young people have in the past  built a peaceful and disciplined  movement that's all the toppling of one  of the world's most autocratic regimes  that of former president umar hassan  al-bashir and as the transition in their  country remains threatened by infighting  and squabbling within the sovereign  council  they're once again finding themselves  trying hard to get their voices heard  these protesters are also angry that  families of those who died during  protests two years ago are yet to get  justice  public discontent has also been mounting  over economic reforms that severely  reduced subsidies on petrol and diesel  more than doubling their price the  sudanese pound runs on several official  and black market rates and is  depreciating causing a loss of cash from  the formal economy these activists say  nothing will stop them achieving a  better way of life and they will bring  hope to a nation that's known little for  decades  mohammed al-jazeera  tom sudan

